  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Acute swelling of the upper lip after eating a prawn soup.\n- Symptoms: Lip swelling starting in the left corner, progressive over ~30 minutes after eating; now more generalized upper lip swelling. Associated throat tightness, difficulty breathing, chest discomfort/feeling of tightness, dizziness. Symptoms worsening during the call.\n  - Onset: ~30 minutes after ingestion of prawn soup (around 7 pm).\n  - Duration: Acute, ongoing at time of call (developed over minutes to an hour).\n  - Severity: Progressively worsening; patient reports breathing difficulty and dizziness (suggesting systemic involvement).\n  - Aggravating factor: Recent ingestion of prawns (seafood). No clear alleviating measures taken prior to call.\n- Past medical history: Asthma. Prior episode ~1 year ago with waking up swollen and breathing difficulty after a late meal/alcohol; childhood severe allergic reaction (mother reported injection and hospital attendance around age 6).\n- Medications / allergies: Uses salbutamol inhaler as needed for asthma. Sometimes takes antihistamines but none currently available. Has a known peanut allergy. No epinephrine auto-injector reported on person.\n- Family / social history: Alone initially at time of call, someone (mother) arrived later. No explicit family history of angioedema documented in the transcript.\n- Vital signs / exam findings: No numerical vitals recorded. Exam findings by patient: visible/progressive upper lip swelling, subjective throat tightness, difficulty breathing, dizziness. Clinician concerned for anaphylaxis.\n- Labs / imaging: None reported.",
